# Three.js Scene Graph Parsing

## Overview
Three.js organizes 3D objects in a scene graph — a tree of `THREE.Object3D` nodes. Understanding this hierarchy is essential for extracting parts from a complex scene.

## Key Concepts

### Object3D Hierarchy
- `THREE.Group` — container node that groups children (no geometry of its own)
- `THREE.Mesh` — leaf node that has geometry + material
- Every Object3D has: `.name`, `.position`, `.rotation`, `.quaternion`, `.scale`, `.children[]`, `.parent`

### Traversing the Scene Graph

```js
// Recursive traversal
root.traverse((child) => {
    if (child.isMesh) {
        console.log(`Mesh: ${child.name}`);
    } else if (child.isGroup) {
        console.log(`Group: ${child.name}`);
    }
});

// Only direct children
root.children.forEach(child => { ... });

// Get world transform (includes all parent transforms)
child.updateWorldMatrix(true, false);
const worldMatrix = child.matrixWorld;
```

### World vs Local Transforms
- `.position`, `.rotation`, `.scale` are **local** (relative to parent)
- `.matrixWorld` gives the **world** transform (accumulated from root)
- Call `object.updateWorldMatrix(true, false)` to ensure matrixWorld is up-to-date
- Apply world matrix to geometry for export: `geometry.applyMatrix4(mesh.matrixWorld)`

### Identifying Parts
- A "part" is typically a `THREE.Group` that contains related meshes
- Meshes directly on the root or on a group form the "link" geometry
- The naming convention in the file (`.name = 'xxx'`) defines the semantic structure

## Pattern: Extracting Part Structure

```js
function getPartStructure(root) {
    const parts = {};
    root.children.forEach(child => {
        if (child.isGroup) {
            parts[child.name] = [];
            child.traverse(grandchild => {
                if (grandchild.isMesh) {
                    parts[child.name].push(grandchild);
                }
            });
        }
    });
    return parts;
}
```

# Three.js Geometry to OBJ Export

## Overview
Exporting Three.js geometries to Wavefront OBJ format requires converting BufferGeometry vertex/face data into the OBJ text format.

## Installation

```bash
npm install three
```

Three.js includes `OBJExporter` in the examples/jsm addons:
```js
import { OBJExporter } from 'three/examples/jsm/exporters/OBJExporter.js';
```

## OBJ File Format Basics

```
# Wavefront OBJ file
o ObjectName
v x y z          # vertex positions
vn nx ny nz      # vertex normals (optional)
vt u v           # texture coordinates (optional)
f v1 v2 v3       # face (1-indexed vertex indices)
f v1//n1 v2//n2  # face with normals
```

## Using OBJExporter

```js
import * as THREE from 'three';
import { OBJExporter } from 'three/examples/jsm/exporters/OBJExporter.js';

const exporter = new OBJExporter();

// Export a single mesh
const scene = new THREE.Scene();
scene.add(mesh);
const objString = exporter.parse(scene);

// Write to file
fs.writeFileSync('output.obj', objString);
```

## Manual OBJ Export (Without OBJExporter)

When finer control is needed, manually convert BufferGeometry:

```js
function geometryToOBJ(geometry, name = 'object') {
    let obj = `o ${name}\n`;
